export default {"format":"cadcraft-help","version":1,"app":"cadcraft","addon":"windows","name":"Windows and doors","sections":{"start":"Getting started","tasks":"Walkthroughs","switching":"Coming from other software","reference":"Reference","trouble":"Troubleshooting and limits"},"topics":[{"slug":"windows-start","scope":"addon:windows","title":"Windows and doors: place and lay out","summary":"Place a window or door by its frame size, set its layout of panes and openings, choose the profile system and colour.","section":"tasks","order":1,"keywords":["window","door","pvc","aluminium","aluminum","profile","frame","sash","mullion","transom","tilt and turn","layout","elevation"],"headings":[{"id":"place-a-window","text":"Place a window","level":2},{"id":"layout","text":"Layout","level":2},{"id":"what-the-sizes-mean","text":"What the sizes mean","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"place-a-window\"\u003EPlace a window\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EType \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EWINDOW\u003C/code\u003E (or \u003Ccode\u003EWIN\u003C/code\u003E). Click the lower left corner of the window&#039;s outer frame; CadCraft draws it in elevation, as seen from the room, with its number and size under it. The prompt shows what the next one will be. Type a letter to change it:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESize\u003C/span\u003E: the outer size of the frame, \u003Ccode\u003E1500x1200\u003C/code\u003E (in the drawing&#039;s units).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayout\u003C/span\u003E: the panes and how they open (below).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESystem\u003C/span\u003E: the profile system (the three that come with the add-on are generic examples). \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EColour\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGlass\u003C/span\u003E (the build as it is written on your order, such as \u003Ccode\u003E4/16/4\u003C/code\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKind\u003C/span\u003E: window or door. A door has a threshold profile along the bottom and a square cut where its sides meet it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPress Enter to finish. Change anything later in Properties: size, layout, system, colour, glass.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"layout\"\u003ELayout\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA layout is written as text. Columns are joined by \u003Ccode\u003E|\u003C/code\u003E; the cells of a column, from the top, by \u003Ccode\u003E/\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEach cell is \u003Ccode\u003EF\u003C/code\u003E fixed light, \u003Ccode\u003EL\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003ER\u003C/code\u003E a turn sash hinged on the left or right, \u003Ccode\u003ETL\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003ETR\u003C/code\u003E a tilt-and-turn sash, \u003Ccode\u003ET\u003C/code\u003E a tilt-only sash.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E~600\u003C/code\u003E after a cell is its height; \u003Ccode\u003E@900\u003C/code\u003E after a column is its width. What is left is shared equally.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ETL|TR\u003C/code\u003E two tilt-and-turn sashes · \u003Ccode\u003EF~600/TL@900|TR\u003C/code\u003E a fixed light over a tilt-and-turn sash in a 900 wide column, then a second sash.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBetween columns there is a mullion; between cells of a column a transom. Each pane gets a sash if it opens, and glass.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-the-sizes-mean\"\u003EWhat the sizes mean\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEverything comes from the profile system&#039;s numbers: the frame&#039;s face width, how far a sash overlaps the frame, how far glass sits in the rebate. See the topics Windows and doors: cut list, bars, glass and labels, and profile systems.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Place a window Type WINDOW (or WIN). Click the lower left corner of the window's outer frame; CadCraft draws it in elevation, as seen from the room, with its number and size under it. The prompt shows what the next one will be. Type a letter to change it: Size: the outer size of the frame, 1500x1200 (in the drawing's units). Layout: the panes and how they open (below). System: the profile system (the three that come with the add-on are generic examples). Colour and Glass (the build as it is written on your order, such as 4/16/4). Kind: window or door. A door has a threshold profile along the bottom and a square cut where its sides meet it. Press Enter to finish. Change anything later in Properties: size, layout, system, colour, glass. Layout A layout is written as text. Columns are joined by |; the cells of a column, from the top, by /. Each cell is F fixed light, L or R a turn sash hinged on the left or right, TL or TR a tilt-and-turn sash, T a tilt-only sash. ~600 after a cell is its height; @900 after a column is its width. What is left is shared equally. TL|TR two tilt-and-turn sashes · F~600/TL@900|TR a fixed light over a tilt-and-turn sash in a 900 wide column, then a second sash. Between columns there is a mullion; between cells of a column a transom. Each pane gets a sash if it opens, and glass. What the sizes mean Everything comes from the profile system's numbers: the frame's face width, how far a sash overlaps the frame, how far glass sits in the rebate. See the topics Windows and doors: cut list, bars, glass and labels, and profile systems."},{"slug":"windows-papers","scope":"addon:windows","title":"Windows and doors: cut list, bars, glass and labels","summary":"The profile cut list with angles and welding allowances, the bars to saw with the least waste, steel, glass sizes, hardware and gaskets, and labels in sawing order.","section":"tasks","order":2,"keywords":["cut list","bars","saw","optimisation","optimization","waste","kerf","mitre","miter","weld","steel","reinforcement","glass","hardware","gasket","labels","csv"],"headings":[{"id":"the-workshop-list","text":"The workshop list","level":2},{"id":"what-the-cut-length-is","text":"What the cut length is","level":2},{"id":"bars","text":"Bars","level":2},{"id":"files","text":"Files","level":2},{"id":"hardware","text":"Hardware","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"the-workshop-list\"\u003EThe workshop list\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EType \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EWINLIST\u003C/code\u003E. It counts the profile pieces, the bars to saw for each profile and colour with the waste, the glass, and the hardware and gaskets. \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EWINBARS\u003C/code\u003E lists every bar: which pieces on it, their angles, and what is left.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-the-cut-length-is\"\u003EWhat the cut length is\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFrame, sash, bead\u003C/strong\u003E are mitred at 45°. The length is the long (outside) edge.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPVC\u003C/strong\u003E corners are welded; each corner melts about 3 mm, so every welded end is cut 1.5 mm longer. Aluminium has no allowance. Beads are clipped in and are never welded.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMullions and transoms\u003C/strong\u003E are cut square (90°) to the opening they sit in.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESteel\u003C/strong\u003E: every PVC frame or sash profile longer than the system&#039;s limit, and every mullion and transom, gets a steel reinforcement, shorter than the profile by a set-back at each end.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"bars\"\u003EBars\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPieces are cut from stock bars (6.5 m for the example PVC systems, 6 m steel and aluminium) with the system&#039;s saw cut and the bar ends trimmed. Each profile and colour is optimised on its own, trying every way a small job can be cut (the lower bound on bars is shown when the search finishes: if it says the result is not proven, the best found in the time allowed is used). The setting \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShare mitre cuts between pieces on a bar\u003C/span\u003E lets two mitred ends that meet be one cut, which saves the profile&#039;s face width for each such pair; it is a way of sawing (the operator turns every other piece), so it is off by default.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"files\"\u003EFiles\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EEXPORT\u003C/code\u003E writes \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWindow profile cut list (CSV)\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWindow bars to saw (CSV)\u003C/span\u003E (every cut with its position on the bar), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWindow glass list (CSV)\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWindow hardware list (CSV)\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWindow labels (HTML, print at 100 %)\u003C/span\u003E. The labels carry the cut length, the angles, the bar and cut number, and a Code 128 bar code of the piece id; they come in sawing order.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"hardware\"\u003EHardware\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPer sash: a handle, hinges (two, three over a set height) or a tilt-and-turn set, locking points (one for about each 1.1 m of sash perimeter, at least two) and as many strikers. Per pane: glazing packers and gasket. Per frame: gasket; per mullion or transom end: a connector. The counts are a starting point: check them against your hardware maker&#039;s tables.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"The workshop list Type WINLIST. It counts the profile pieces, the bars to saw for each profile and colour with the waste, the glass, and the hardware and gaskets. WINBARS lists every bar: which pieces on it, their angles, and what is left. What the cut length is Frame, sash, bead are mitred at 45°. The length is the long (outside) edge. PVC corners are welded; each corner melts about 3 mm, so every welded end is cut 1.5 mm longer. Aluminium has no allowance. Beads are clipped in and are never welded. Mullions and transoms are cut square (90°) to the opening they sit in. Steel: every PVC frame or sash profile longer than the system's limit, and every mullion and transom, gets a steel reinforcement, shorter than the profile by a set-back at each end. Bars Pieces are cut from stock bars (6.5 m for the example PVC systems, 6 m steel and aluminium) with the system's saw cut and the bar ends trimmed. Each profile and colour is optimised on its own, trying every way a small job can be cut (the lower bound on bars is shown when the search finishes: if it says the result is not proven, the best found in the time allowed is used). The setting Share mitre cuts between pieces on a bar lets two mitred ends that meet be one cut, which saves the profile's face width for each such pair; it is a way of sawing (the operator turns every other piece), so it is off by default. Files EXPORT writes Window profile cut list (CSV), Window bars to saw (CSV) (every cut with its position on the bar), Window glass list (CSV), Window hardware list (CSV) and Window labels (HTML, print at 100 %). The labels carry the cut length, the angles, the bar and cut number, and a Code 128 bar code of the piece id; they come in sawing order. Hardware Per sash: a handle, hinges (two, three over a set height) or a tilt-and-turn set, locking points (one for about each 1.1 m of sash perimeter, at least two) and as many strikers. Per pane: glazing packers and gasket. Per frame: gasket; per mullion or transom end: a connector. The counts are a starting point: check them against your hardware maker's tables."},{"slug":"windows-systems","scope":"addon:windows","title":"Windows and doors: profile systems","summary":"What a profile system holds, the generic example systems that come with the add-on, and how to add your own.","section":"reference","order":3,"keywords":["profile system","profiles","pvc","aluminium","aluminum","thermal break","weld","steel","glazing bead","rebate","overlap","own system","json"],"headings":[{"id":"the-generic-examples","text":"The generic examples","level":2},{"id":"what-a-system-holds","text":"What a system holds","level":2},{"id":"add-your-own","text":"Add your own","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"the-generic-examples\"\u003EThe generic examples\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe add-on brings three systems: Generic PVC 70 mm, Generic PVC 60 mm and Generic aluminium 50 mm with thermal break. \u003Cstrong\u003EThey are examples with round, plausible numbers, not any maker&#039;s profile data.\u003C/strong\u003E Before you cut anything, put in your own system with your maker&#039;s real numbers.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-a-system-holds\"\u003EWhat a system holds\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EProfiles: \u003Cstrong\u003Eframe, sash, mullion, bead\u003C/strong\u003E (and a \u003Cstrong\u003Ethreshold\u003C/strong\u003E for doors), each with a code, a face width and a depth. Frame and sash also have a \u003Cstrong\u003Eseat\u003C/strong\u003E: how far the glass sits in the rebate beyond the sight line.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESash overlap\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003Esash gap\u003C/strong\u003E: a sash is its pane plus 2 × (overlap − gap).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EGlass gap\u003C/strong\u003E: the glass is the sash&#039;s sight line plus 2 × (seat − gap).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBead less\u003C/strong\u003E: a bead&#039;s long edge is the glass edge less this much.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EWeld\u003C/strong\u003E: what a welded PVC corner melts in all (0 for aluminium).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBar\u003C/strong\u003E: stock length, saw cut, and the trim taken off both ends.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESteel\u003C/strong\u003E: code, the set-back at each end, and the length over which a profile takes steel.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EHardware\u003C/strong\u003E: one locking point per this much sash perimeter; the sash height over which a turn sash takes a third hinge; packers per pane.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EColours\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"add-your-own\"\u003EAdd your own\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWindows and doors\u003C/span\u003E, paste a JSON list into \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EYour own profile systems\u003C/span\u003E. A minimum system:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E[{ &quot;id&quot;: &quot;my-62&quot;, &quot;name&quot;: &quot;Shop PVC 62&quot;,\n   &quot;profiles&quot;: { &quot;frame&quot;: { &quot;code&quot;: &quot;F62&quot;, &quot;w&quot;: 62, &quot;depth&quot;: 62, &quot;seat&quot;: 13 },\n                 &quot;sash&quot;: { &quot;code&quot;: &quot;S70&quot;, &quot;w&quot;: 70, &quot;depth&quot;: 70, &quot;seat&quot;: 14 },\n                 &quot;bead&quot;: { &quot;code&quot;: &quot;B20&quot;, &quot;w&quot;: 20, &quot;depth&quot;: 20 } } }]\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAnything left out takes the example system&#039;s value. A window made with your system keeps a copy of it, so the file opens on a computer that does not have it. Use a maker&#039;s real numbers only with the maker&#039;s permission.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"The generic examples The add-on brings three systems: Generic PVC 70 mm, Generic PVC 60 mm and Generic aluminium 50 mm with thermal break. They are examples with round, plausible numbers, not any maker's profile data. Before you cut anything, put in your own system with your maker's real numbers. What a system holds Profiles: frame, sash, mullion, bead (and a threshold for doors), each with a code, a face width and a depth. Frame and sash also have a seat: how far the glass sits in the rebate beyond the sight line. Sash overlap and sash gap: a sash is its pane plus 2 × (overlap − gap). Glass gap: the glass is the sash's sight line plus 2 × (seat − gap). Bead less: a bead's long edge is the glass edge less this much. Weld: what a welded PVC corner melts in all (0 for aluminium). Bar: stock length, saw cut, and the trim taken off both ends. Steel: code, the set-back at each end, and the length over which a profile takes steel. Hardware: one locking point per this much sash perimeter; the sash height over which a turn sash takes a third hinge; packers per pane. Colours. Add your own In Settings › Windows and doors, paste a JSON list into Your own profile systems. A minimum system: [{ \"id\": \"my-62\", \"name\": \"Shop PVC 62\", \"profiles\": { \"frame\": { \"code\": \"F62\", \"w\": 62, \"depth\": 62, \"seat\": 13 }, \"sash\": { \"code\": \"S70\", \"w\": 70, \"depth\": 70, \"seat\": 14 }, \"bead\": { \"code\": \"B20\", \"w\": 20, \"depth\": 20 } } }] Anything left out takes the example system's value. A window made with your system keeps a copy of it, so the file opens on a computer that does not have it. Use a maker's real numbers only with the maker's permission."}]};
